            "explanation": "Arabic is written right-to-left, and it has its own 28-letter alphabet — we'll pick that up gradually across this unit rather than all at once, starting instead with two greetings you'll use constantly. مرحباً (marhaban) is the all-purpose, everywhere-and-anytime \"hello,\" casual enough for a shopkeeper and polite enough for a stranger. السلام عليكم (as-salāmu ʿalaykum, literally \"peace be upon you\") is the more traditional greeting used across the entire Arab world, by speakers of every religion, in almost every context — and it comes with a near-mandatory reply: وعليكم السلام (wa ʿalaykum as-salām, \"and upon you, peace\"). Leaving that reply unsaid feels as jarring as ignoring someone's \"hello\" outright. Add صباح الخير (sabāḥ al-khayr, good morning) with its reply صباح النور (sabāḥ an-nūr, literally \"morning of light\") and you have everything you need to open a conversation with today's two characters, عمر (Omar) and سارة (Sarah).",
