        "presentation": {
            "explanation": "The **Iḍāfa (الإِضَافَة)** is the premier grammatical engine of Arabic possession, relationship, and compound naming. It translates the English 'of' or apostrophe-s (e.g. 'the father's house' or 'the house of the father').\n\n1. **The Iḍāfa Formula (Term 1 + Term 2):**\n   - **Term 1 (المُضَاف - al-Muḍāf / The Annexed Noun):**\n     * The object or person being possessed (e.g. *bayt* = house, *sayyārah* = car).\n     * **RULE 1:** It NEVER takes the definite article **الـ (al-)**!\n     * **RULE 2:** It NEVER takes **tanwīn** (-un, -an, -in)!\n     * It is made automatically definite through its relationship with Term 2.\n   - **Term 2 (المُضَاف إِلَيْه - al-Muḍāf Ilayh / The Possessor):**\n     * The owner or specifier (e.g. *al-wālid* = the father, *aṭ-ṭālib* = the student).\n     * **RULE 3:** It takes the **Genitive Case (al-Majrūr)**, marked by a **kasra (-i)** or genitive ending!\n\n2. **Concrete Examples:**\n   - 'The father's house' / 'The house of the father':\n     * **بَيْتُ الوَالِدِ** (*baytu al-wālidi* [BAY-too al-WAH-lee-dee])\n     * (Notice: *baytu* has no 'al-' and no tanwīn; *al-wālidi* ends with genitive kasra!).\n   - 'The teacher's car':\n     * **سَيَّارَةُ المُعَلِّمِ** (*sayyāratu al-muʿallimi*)\n   - 'The university library':\n     * **مَكْتَبَةُ الجَامِعَةِ** (*maktabatu al-jāmiʿati*)\n   - 'My sister's book':\n     * **كِتَابُ أُخْتِي** (*kitābu ukhtī*)\n\n3. **Indefinite Iḍāfa:**\n   - If Term 2 is indefinite (with tanwīn kasr -in), the entire phrase is indefinite:\n     * **بَيْتُ رَجُلٍ** (*baytu rajulin* = a house of a man / a man's house).",
            "examples": [
                "بَيْتُ أَبِي كَبِيرٌ. (Baytu abī kabīrun / My father's house is big).",
                "سَيَّارَةُ مُحَمَّدٍ جَدِيدَةٌ. (Sayyāratu Muḥammadin jadīdatun / Muhammad's car is new).",
                "جَامِعَةُ القَاهِرَةِ قَدِيمَةٌ. (Jāmiʿatu al-Qāhirati qadīmatun / Cairo University is old).",
                "هَذَا مِفْتَاحُ البَيْتِ. (Hādhā miftāḥu al-bayti / This is the key of the house)."
            ],
            "mnemonics": [
                "Iḍāfa Rule: Term 1 is NAKED (no 'al-', no tanwīn). Term 2 wears the Genitive KASRA (-i).",
                "Think of Term 1 as borrowing its definiteness directly from Term 2."
            ],
            "culturalNotes": [
                "Many historic Arab universities and landmarks are named as Iḍāfa constructs: Jāmi'at Dimashq (University of Damascus), Bayt al-Ḥikmah (House of Wisdom in Baghdad), Madīnat al-Kuwayt (Kuwait City)."
            ]
        },
