        "presentation": {
            "explanation": "Expanding present-tense conjugation to **Plural Persons (الجَمْع - al-Jamʿ)** introduces the **Nūn Prefix (نَـ)** and the **-ūna Suffix (ـُونَ)**.\n\n1. **The Plural Present-Tense Conjugation Matrix:**\n- **نَحْنُ (We):** **نَكْتُبُ** (*naḥnu naktub**u*** [nah-NOO NAK-too-boo]) -> Prefix **na- (نَـ)** + suffix **-u**\n- **أَنْتُمْ (You masc plural):** **تَكْتُبُونَ** (*'antum taktub**ūna*** [ahn-TOOM tak-too-BOO-nah]) -> Prefix **ta- (تَـ)** + suffix **-ūna (ـُونَ)**\n- **هُمْ (They masc plural):** **يَكْتُبُونَ** (*hum yaktub**ūna*** [HOOM yak-too-BOO-nah]) -> Prefix **ya- (يَـ)** + suffix **-ūna (ـُونَ)**\n\n2. **Conjugating 'Study' (نَدْرُسُ) & 'Work' (نَعْمَلُ) in the Plural:**\n- **naḥnu:** *nadrusu* (we study) / *na'malu* (we work)\n- **'antum:** *tadrusūna* (you pl study) / *ta'malūna* (you pl work)\n- **hum:** *yadrusūna* (they study) / *ya'malūna* (they work).\n\n3. **The Dual (المُثَنَّى - al-Muthannā) Overview:**\n- Arabic uniquely has a specific dual form for exactly TWO people:\n  * **هُمَا (The two of them):** **يَكْتُبَانِ** (*humā yaktubāni* [HOO-mah yak-too-BAH-nee]) -> Prefix **ya-** + dual suffix **-āni (ـَانِ)**.",
            "examples": [
                "نَحْنُ نَدْرُسُ اللُّغَةَ العَرَبِيَّةَ مَعاً. (naḥnu nadrusu al-lughata al-'arabiyyata ma'an - We study Arabic together).",
                "هَلْ تَعْمَلُونَ فِي المَكْتَب؟ (hal ta'malūna fī al-maktab? - Do you pl work in the office?).",
                "الطُّلابُ يَدْرُسُونَ فِي المَكْتَبَة. (aṭ-ṭullābu yadrusūna fī al-maktaba - The students study in the library).",
                "الطَّالِبَانِ يَكْتُبَانِ الدَّرْس. (aṭ-ṭālibāni yaktubāni ad-dars - The two students write the lesson)."
            ],
            "mnemonics": [
                "naḥnu = na- (naḥnu naktubu = We write).",
                "Plural -ūna: 'antum taktub-ŪNA, hum yaktub-ŪNA.",
                "Dual -āni: humā yaktub-ĀNI (2 people)."
            ],
            "culturalNotes": [
                "In contemporary spoken Arabic dialects ('Āmmiyya), plural forms (*taktubū / yaktubū*) drop the final '-na' sound, but preserving '-ūna' is essential in Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) media and literature."
            ]
        },
