            "explanation": "Elia Suleiman (1960–present - Nazareth & Paris/New York) and the supreme deadpan burlesque cinema of Palestinian existentialism:\n- **Elia Suleiman (إيليا سليمان / \"The Palestinian Buster Keaton\")**:\n  - The most stylistically distinct auteur in contemporary Arab cinema; crafts silent, deadpan, static-frame choreographies that dissect the absurdity of military occupation, borders, and human surveillance with subtle comedic poetry.\n- Stylistic Mastery & Cinematic Philosophy:\n  - **The Silent Mute Observer (E.S.)**:\n    - Suleiman plays the silent central protagonist (E.S.) who observes the world without speaking a word, mirroring the deadpan physical comedy of **Buster Keaton** and **Jacques Tati**;\n  - **The Military Checkpoint as Metaphysical Theater (الحاجز العسكري مسرحاً وجودياً)**:\n    - Checkpoints and border walls are transformed from realistic battlefields into absurd, surreal stages where bored soldiers and ordinary citizens engage in surreal ballets of mutual estrangement;\n- Monumental Masterpieces:\n  - **«سجل اختفاء» (Chronicle of a Disappearance - 1996 - Venice Best First Film Prize)**: Explores the state of limbo in Nazareth and Jerusalem;\n  - **«يد إلهية» (Divine Intervention - 2002 - Cannes Jury Prize & FIPRESCI Prize)**:\n    - Features the iconic surreal fantasy sequence of a Palestinian ninja-heroine deflecting bullets with a keffiyeh and exploding an Israeli tank with an apricot pit;\n  - **«الزمن الباقي» (The Time That Remains - 2009)** & **«إن شئت كما في السماء» (It Must Be Heaven - 2019 - Cannes Special Mention)**.",
