            "explanation": "The Levantine Mezze (المزة الشامية - Lebanon, Syria, Palestine, Jordan) and the art of communal abundance:\n- **The Philosophy of the Mezze (فلسفة المزة والسفرة الشامية)**:\n  - Derived from the Arabic root *mazza* (to savor, sip, and nibble slowly);\n  - A dazzling table of 30 to 50 colorful small dishes designed for prolonged, unhurried conversation, breaking bread together, and honoring the guest (*Ikrām al-Dayf*);\n- Master Pillar Dishes & Botanical Terroir:\n  - **Tabbouleh (التبولة)**:\n    - The undisputed queen of Levantine salads;\n    - 90% hand-chopped flat-leaf parsley, mint, red tomatoes, scallions, a tiny pinch of fine bulgur wheat, seasoned with freshly squeezed lemon juice and **Cold-Pressed Extra Virgin Olive Oil (زيت الزيتون البكر الممتاز)**;\n  - **Fattoush (الفتوش)**: Crisp rustic salad brightened by wild sumac (*Summāq*), purslane (*Baqleh*), pomegranate molasses (*Dibs Rummān*), and crispy fried pita chips;\n  - **Hummus bi-Tahina (الحمص بالطحينة)**: Silky puréed chickpeas blended with sesame tahini paste, garlic, and lemon juice, topped with olive oil and pine nuts;\n  - **Mutabbal / Baba Ghanoush (المتبل)**: Fire-roasted eggplants infused with smoky charcoal aroma, tahini, and garlic;\n  - **Kibbeh (الكبة)**: Finely pounded lamb and bulgur wheat kneaded in a stone mortar (*Jurn*), served raw (*Kibbeh Nayyeh*), fried, or grilled over charcoal with walnuts and spices.",
